\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Observation and action interface}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{agent}

\p{For task #{q}, let #{\mathcal O_q} be the set of observations available to
the policy and #{\mathcal A_q} the set of actions accepted by the environment.
The pair #{(\mathcal O_q,\mathcal A_q)} is the \strong{interaction interface}.}

\p{The observation/action distinction is standard in partially observable
control; see \citef{kaelbling1998planning}, Section 2. FTIP keeps both spaces
task-indexed because a proof assistant, web browser, and code sandbox expose
different legal actions and evidence.}

\p{An action may be a token, an utterance, a structured tool call, or a patch.
An observation may contain text, a test result, or a structured state summary.
The interface definition fixes the type; it does not yet specify the dynamics.}
